==English==
{{was wotd|2017|August|21}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|accultation||rare}}

===Etymology===
[[File:Solar eclipse 1999 4.jpg|thumb|The occultation of the [[Sun]] by the [[Moon]] during the [[w:Solar eclipse of August 11, 1999|solar eclipse of 11 August 1999]]. A total [[eclipse#Noun|eclipse]] was visible mainly in [[Europe]].]]

From {{der|en|la|occultātiōnem}}, {{glossary|accusative case|accusative}} {{glossary|singular number|singular}} of {{m|la|occultātiō||concealment; insinuation}}, from {{m|la|occultāre}}, {{glossary|present tense|present}} {{glossary|active voice|active}} {{glossary|infinitive}} of {{m|la|occultō||to conceal, hide}};<ref>Compare {{R:Online Etymology Dictionary}}</ref> analysable as {{suffix|en|occult|ation}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌɒkəlˈteɪʃ(ə)n/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˌɑkəlˈteɪʃ(ə)n/|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-occultation.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{hyphenation|en|oc|cul|ta|tion}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|astronomy}} An [[astronomical]] [[event]] that occurs when one [[celestial object]] is [[hidden]] by another celestial object that passes between it and the [[observer]] when the nearer object appears larger and completely hides the more distant object.
#: {{syn|en|eclipse}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=John Flamstead [''i.e.'', {{w|John Flamsteed}}]|title=An Accompt of such of the More Notable Celestial Phænomena of the Year 1670, as will be Conspicuous in the English Horizon; Written by the Learned and Industrious Mr. John Flamstead Novemb. 4 1669. and by Him Addressed and Recommended for Encouragement, to the Right Honorable, the Lord {{w|Viscount Brouncker}}, as President of the Royal Society|journal=[[w:Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society|Philosophical Transactions: Giving Some Accompt of the Present Undertakings, Studies and Labours of the Ingenious in Many Considerable Parts of the World]]|location=London|publisher=Printed for T. N. for [[w:John Martyn (publisher)|John Martyn]] at the Bell, a little without [[w:Temple Bar, London|Temple-Bar]], printer to the {{w|Royal Society}}|date=17 January 1670|volume=IV|issue=55|pages=1101–1102|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=7sFeAAAAcAAJ&pg=PP221|oclc=644029022|passage=[T]he diſtance of any place within this kingdom from it, will not much vary the manner of their Appearance in any of the ''Phænomena'', except the Eclipſe of the Sun: for, in the '''Occultations''', the Stars will appeare to paſs nearly under the ſame Angles and Spots of the Moon; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Melville Dozier|title=[Transactions for November. III. Meetings of Sections.] 2. Section of Astronomy|journal=Bulletin of the Southern California Academy of Sciences|location=Los Angeles, Calif.|publisher=Published for the Association by R. Baumgardt & Co. 231 [[w:1st Street, Los Angeles|West First St.]], Los Angeles, Cal.|month=December|year=1904|volume=III|issue=9|page=147|pageurl=https://archive.org/stream/bulletinofsouthe0103sout#page/147/mode/1up/|oclc=921919756|passage=The bright star Aldebaran is to be occulted by the moon on December 20th, at about 5 o'clock pm. Aldebaran is said to be eight hundred and eighty times the mass of the sun, with a diameter of over 8,000,000 miles; a distance so great that a meteor traveling at the rate of thirty miles per second, would require over three days to cross the disk of the star. Yet, notwithstanding the immense volume, the '''accultation''' will occur in a moment, so great is the distance of the star from us, and will continue for about one hour and eight minutes.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=C. B. Olkin; [[w:James L. Elliot|J[ames] L[udlow] Elliot]]|chapter=Occultation Astrometry: Predictions and Post-event Results|editors=L. V. Morrison; G. F. Gilmore|title=Galactic and Solar System Optical Astrometry: Proceedings of the [[w:Royal Observatory, Greenwich|Royal Greenwich Observatory]] and the [[w:Institute of Astronomy, Cambridge|Institute of Astronomy]] workshop held in Cambridge, June 21–24, 1993|location=Cambridge|publisher=w:Cambridge University Press|year=1994|section=abstract|page=286|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=XBnYSPVKmNAC&pg=PA286|isbn=978-0-521-46240-2|passage=Stellar '''occultations''' potentially provide the highest precision data for relating solar system ephemerides to the stellar reference frame. For example, '''occultations''' by the Uranian rings can define the position of the occulted star relative to the rings to better than 0.02 mas (equivalent to a few hundred meters at the distance of Uranus). '''Occultations''' by atmospheres can be less precise than '''occultations''' by symmerical solid bodies, like rings and large asteroids, with a precision on the order of 1.0 mas.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:David H. Levy|chapter=When the Moon Occults a Star or a Planet|title=David Levy’s Guide to Eclipses, Occultations, and Transits|location=Cambridge; New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Cambridge University Press|year=2010|page=129|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=6jT3N7jnWwYC&pg=PA119|isbn=978-0-521-16551-8|passage={{...}} I was thrilled at the prospect of seeing the waning crescent Moon swing over Venus in the predawn sky on Wednesday morning, April 22, 2009. While the '''occultation''' was visible from much of North America, it was only in Arizona and parts of other surrounding states, where Wendee and I live, that the ingress would take place in a completely dark sky. It would be a highlight of the International Year of Astronomy, which reached its peak during 2009.}}
# The state of being {{l|en|occult||[[hidden]], [[undetected]]}}.
#: {{syn|en|hiddenness|invisibility|occultness}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=[[w:Francis Jeffrey, Lord Jeffrey|[Francis Jeffrey, Lord Jeffrey]]]|title=Art. I. ''Theodric, a Domestic Tale: With other Poems.'' By {{smallcaps|Thomas Campbell}}. 12mo. pp. 150. London, 1824.|magazine=[[w:Edinburgh Review|The Edinburgh Review, or Critical Journal]]|location=Edinburgh|publisher=Printed by the heirs of D[avid] Willison, for [[w:Constable & Robinson|Archibald Constable and Company]], Edinburgh; and [[w:Longman|Longman, Hurst, Rees, Orme, Brown and Green]], London|month=January|year=1825|volume=XLI|issue=LXXXII|oclc=925538131|passage=If Mr. [[w:Thomas Campbell (poet)|[Thomas] Campbell]]'s poetry was of a kind that could be forgotten, his long fits of silence would put him fairly in the way of that misfortune. {{...}} [T]he re-appearance of such an author, after those long periods of '''occultation''', is naturally hailed as a novelty—and he receives the double welcome of a celebrated stranger and a remembered friend.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Thomas M. Traill|editors=Thomas Anderson; [[w:Sir William Jardine, 7th Baronet|William Jardine]]; w:John Hutton Balfour; [[w:Henry Darwin Rogers|Henry D[arwin] Rogers]]|title=On the Occultation of Rivers|journal=[[w:Royal Society of Edinburgh|The Edinburgh New Philosophical Journal, Exhibiting a View of the Progressive Discoveries and Improvements in the Sciences and the Arts]]|location=Edinburgh|publisher=[[w:A & C Black|Adam and Charles Black]]; London: [[w:Longman|Longman, Brown, Green, & Longmans]]|month=July|year=1857|volume=VI|issue=1 (New Series)|page=41|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=S4B1C5lEkQcC&pg=PA41|oclc=926996861|passage=In western Europe, the most celebrated instance of the '''occultation''' of a river ''was'' that of the Rhone, near Bellegarde, on the confines of France and Savoy, about 18 miles from Geneva; but, alas, on visiting this interesting spot in the autumn of 1855, I found that the ''Perte du Rhone'' no longer exists; for the Sardinian Government, in which territory it was, had blown up the rocky roof, beneath which it had been engulfed, in order to facilitate the conveyance of timber on the river.}}
# {{lb|en|Shia}} The [[disappearance]] of the Twelfth Imam, or [[Mahdi]], who is believed alive and present in this world, but hidden until his reappearance at the end of time.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Sa'id Akhtar Rizvi|Allamah Sayyid Saeed Akhtar Rizvi]]|title=Prophecies about Occultation of Imãm Al-Mahdi (A.S.)|location=Dar es Salaam, Tanzania|publisher=[[w:Bilal Muslim Mission|Bilal Muslim Mission of Tanzania]]|year=2002|page=7|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=gJurAgAAQBAJ&pg=PA7|isbn=978-9987-620-23-4|passage=We are therefore, constantly hoping for the joy and happiness, personified in [[w:Muhammad al-Mahdi|Imām al-Mahdi]], the Imām who is alive at this very time, and who is himself awaiting the command of Allāh to reappear, so that he may strengthen the weak, and judge the oppressors. He will reappear "without having done ''Bay'ah'' to any oppressor". He went into '''occultation''' because he did not want [to] submit to any unjust ruler.}}

