# [[present|Present]]; [[current]].
#* '''1630''' November 19, Alexander Every, will quoted in '''1887''', Frederick Arthur Crisp, ''Abstracts of Somersetshire Wills Etc: Copied from the Manuscript Collections of the Late Rev. Frederick Brown, M.A., F.S.A.'', page 75:
#*: {{...}}to my '''now''' wife Mabell, my child Elisabeth, & my child '''now''' begotten, &c., and they to be joint Exõrs, & if all die, then the same jointly to my brother William Every & my sisters Sarah Skibbowe & Joan Brice.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=17th century|author=w:Joseph Glanvill|title=Scepsis Scientifica: Or, Confest Ignorance, the Way to Science; in an Essay of the Vanity of Dogmatizing and Confident Opinion|year_published=1885|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=3jU0AQAAMAAJ&q=%22Our+now+happiness%22+Glanvill&dq=%22Our+now+happiness%22+Glanvill&hl=en&sa=X&ei=WSHqUbm2MsXqiAeS3YCwBQ&redir_esc=y|page=207|passage=Defects seem as necessary to our '''now''' happiness as their Opposites.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1855|author=Conrad Swackhamer|title=The United States democratic review|volume=5|passage=The history of the infant colonies teaches us that the country comprised within the limits of the '''now''' United States of America was originally patented in the reign of James I., of England, into ''two portions:'' that in less than eighty years from that period, the same was again divided into ''twelve'' distinct provinces; a ''thirteenth'' being after added in the creation of the State of Georgia.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1908|title=The English reports|passage=Where in assumpsit for money lent, the defendant pleaded that in an action in which the '''now''' defendant was plaintiff, and the '''now''' plaintiff was defendant,{{...}}.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2010-03-17|work=w:The Daily Telegraph|url=http://www.telegraph.co.uk/culture/tvandradio/bbc/7457761/Radio-4-apologises-for-day-old-shipping-forecast.html|title=Radio 4 apologises for day old shipping forecast|passage=Radio 4's continuity announcer said at the end of the show: "As many of you will have noticed, that edition of The Now Show wasn't very '''now'''. It was actually last week's programme. Our apologies for that."|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261007200008/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/culture/tvandradio/bbc/7457761/Radio-4-apologises-for-day-old-shipping-forecast.html|archivedate=7 October 2026}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Mike Howell|title=Partnership With ADL Compromises FBI’s Integrity and Fairness|magazine=w:The Daily Signal|url=https://www.dailysignal.com/2024/02/16/time-end-fbi-adl-partnership/|date=2024-02-16|passage=For example, the organization has groundlessly denounced Elon Musk, a powerful libertarian voice, as an antisemite in a baldfaced effort to drain his social network, X, of advertising revenue. Yet, when '''now'''-Minority Leader Hakeem Jeffries (D-N.Y.) came to the defense of his uncle for antisemitic remarks he made in the ’90s, the organization refused to condemn him.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260824170520/https://www.dailysignal.com/2024/02/16/time-end-fbi-adl-partnership/|archivedate=24 August 2026}}
# {{lb|en|informal}} [[fashionable|Fashionable]]; [[popular]]; [[up to date]]; [[current]].
#: {{ux|en|I think this band's sound is very '''now'''.}}
#* {{quote-av
|en
|date=February 21, 1969
|quotee=[[w:Charles Napier (actor)|Charles Napier]]
|title=w:The Way to Eden
|series={{w|Star Trek: The Original Series}}
|publisher=w:Paramount Domestic Television
|oclc=42867221
|passage=ADAM: I was just thinking, I... ''(sees the lyre)'' Hey, brother, do you play? Is it Vulcan? Can I try it? // ''(Spock gestures his permission and Adam strums a chord)'' // ADAM: Ho ho, that's '''now'''! That's real '''now'''! I reach that, brother. I really do. // ''(He hands the lyre to Spock who plays it briefly)''}}
#* {{quote-av|en|medium=television production|year=2000|title=Black Books|episode=Cooking the Books|number=1|season=1|passage=Bernard: What does it do?<br>Fran: It's very in.<br>Bernard: You don't know what it is, do you?<br>Fran: It's very '''now'''.}}

