====Adverb====
{{en-adv|-}}

# At the present [[time]].
#: {{ux|en|'''Now''' I am six.}}
#: {{ux|en|Stop that '''now''', Jimmy!}}
#: {{ux|en|I could have been Secretary of State '''now''' if I hadn’t left politics five years ago.}}
#: {{ux|en|We can’t phone '''now''' – they will certainly have gone to bed.}}
# {{lb|en|[[sentential]]}} {{non-gloss|Used to introduce a point, a [[qualification]] of what has previously been said, a [[remonstration]] or a [[rebuke]].}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Now''', we all want what is best for our children.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Now''', Jimmy, stop that.}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|page=43|passage='''Now''' all this was very fine, but not at all in keeping with the Celebrity's character as I had come to conceive it. The idea that adulation ever cloyed on him was ludicrous in itself. In fact I thought the whole story fishy, and came very near to saying so.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1949|author=Isabella Taves|title=That Unpredictable Look|journal=w:The American Magazine|volume=CXLVII|location=Springfield, Oh.|publisher=[[w:Crowell-Collier Publishing Company|The Crowell-Collier Publishing Company]]|page=38|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=IZxXAAAAMAAJ&q=%22Now,+Harry%22|issn=2155-7225|oclc=6403725|passage=“I don’t stare at her,” Steve insisted quickly. “It’s just that every once in a while she is looking at me when I look out the window.” / “A [[peeping Thomasina]].” / “'''Now''', Harry.” / “Well, it’s her name. Elizabeth Thomas.”}}
# [[differently|Differently]] from the immediate past; differently from a more remote past or a possible future; differently from all other times.
#: {{ux|en|'''Now''' I am ready.}}
#: {{ux|en|We all '''now''' want the latest toys for our children.}}
#: {{ux|en|We all want what is '''now''' best for our children.}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=5|passage=Although the Celebrity was almost impervious to sarcasm, he was '''now''' beginning to exhibit visible signs of uneasiness, the consciousness dawning upon him that his eccentricity was not receiving the ovation it merited.}}
# At the time reached within a narration.
#: {{ux|en|'''Now''' he remembered why he had come.}}
#: {{ux|en|He '''now''' asked her whether she had made pudding.}}
#: {{ux|en|The pudding was '''now''' ready to be served.}}
# {{non-gloss|Used to indicate a context of urgency.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Now''' listen, we must do something about this.}}
# {{lb|en|informal}} At the [[present]] [[point]] of a [[recurring]] [[cycle]] or [[event]].
#: {{ux|en|I always used to do my shopping '''now''', to avoid the rush.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|as '''''[[but]] now'''''}} Very recently; not long ago; up to the present.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=c. 1656|author=w:Edmund Waller|title=Of a War with Spain, and Fight for Sea
|passage=They that '''but now''', for honour and for plate, / Made the sea blush with blood, resign their hate.}}
# {{lb|en|usually|emphasized}} {{n-g|Used to address a switching side, or sharp change in attitude from before.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Now''', you want to protect me. An hour ago, you were mercilessly bullying me!}}
# Sometimes; occasionally; {{ng|used to list a series of often assumed states}}.
#: {{ux|en|His face fit his roles: '''now''' smiling, '''now''' earnest, '''now''' glowering, '''now''' raging.}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Great Expectations|I|V|63|And then they stood about, as soldiers do; '''now''', with their hands loosely clasped before them; '''now''', resting a knee or a shoulder; '''now''', easing a belt or a pouch; '''now''', opening the door to spit stiffly over their high stocks, out into the yard.}}

