====Usage notes====
When referring to astronomical orbits, "in orbit" and "on orbit" have somewhat different meanings. In general, a body is said to be "in orbit" if it is in freefall going around another body; while something happens "on orbit"{{rfv-sense|en}} if it occurs aboard an orbiting spacecraft. Thus one might say, "The space capsule is ''in orbit,'' and the astronauts inside are performing experiments ''on orbit.''"

