# The part of something that faces [[out]]; the [[outer]] [[surface]].
#: {{ux|en|He's repainting the '''outside''' of his house.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1653|translator=w:Thomas Urquhart|author=w:François Rabelais|title=[[s:Gargantua/The Author's Prologue to the First Book|Gargantua]]|chapter=The Author's Prologue to the First Book
|passage=Silenes of old swere little boxes, like those we now may see in the shops of apothecaries, painted on the '''outside''' with wanton toyish figures, as harpies, satyrs, bridled geese, horned hares, saddled ducks, flying goats, thiller harts, and other such-like counterfeited pictures at discretion, ...}}
#* {{RQ:Riis Other Half|page=159|passage=The '''outside''' of the building gives no valuable clew.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1911|publisher=[[s:1911 Encyclopædia Britannica/Cab|Cab]]|title=w:Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition
|passage=The number of persons which the cab is licensed to carry must be painted at the back on the '''outside'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1950|month=February|author=W. Dendy|title=Impressions of the Indian Railways—3|journal=Railway Magazine|page=120|text=Third-class carriages are grossly overcrowded, with passengers lying on the luggage racks, standing between the benches, and occasionally even riding on the footboards and clinging to the '''outsides''' of the coaches for short distances.}}
# The [[external]] [[appearance]] of someone or something.
#: {{ux|en|Her '''outside''' was stern, but inside was a heart of gold.}}
# The [[space]] [[beyond]] some [[limit]] or [[boundary]].
#: {{ux|en|Viewed from the '''outside''', the building seemed unremarkable.}}
#* {{RQ:Spectator|VI|577|August 6 1714|author=Addison|passage=I in great Transport threw open the Door of my Chamber, and found the greatest Part of the Family standing on the '''Outside''' in a very great Consternation}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1967|author=w:The Bee Gees|title=w:New York Mining Disaster 1941
|passage=Have you seen my wife, Mr Jones?,Do you know what it's like on the '''outside'''?}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1982|author=w:Anne Dudley; w:Trevor Horn; w:Malcolm Mclaren|title=w:Buffalo Gals
|passage=Four buffalo gals go 'round the '''outside''' <br> 'Round the '''outside''' <br> 'Round the '''outside''' <br> Four buffalo gals go 'round the '''outside''' <br> And do-si-do your partners.}}
# The furthest limit, as to number, quantity, extent, etc.
#: {{ux|en|It may last a week at the '''outside'''.}}
# The part of a road towards the central division: towards the right if one drives on the left, or towards the left if one drives on the right.
#: {{ux|en|On a motorway, you should always overtake other vehicles on the '''outside'''.}}
# The side of a curved road, racetrack etc. that has the longer arc length; the side of a racetrack furthest from the interior of the course or some other point of reference.
#: {{ux|en|On the final bend, the second-place car tried to go around the '''outside''' of the leader but spun off into the barrier.}}
# {{lb|en|surfing}} The outer part of the sea, away from the [[peak]] of a [[wave]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2011|author=Rick Hansen|title=Leadership and The Art of Surfing|page=xi
|passage=When a wave mounds on the '''outside''' and takes its shape, a surfer quickly paddles to the peak, positions himself in its evolving momentum, swings his board around, aligns with the peak, and thrusts himself into its cascading shape.}}
# {{lb|en|dated|UK|colloquial}} A [[passenger]] riding on the outside of a [[coach]] or [[carriage]].
#* {{RQ:Dickens Pickwick Papers|passage=The '''outsides''' did as '''outsides''' always do. They were very cheerful and talkative at the beginning of every stage, and very dismal and sleepy in the middle{{...}}}}

