===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# One of a set of [[choice]]s that can be made. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|date=October 23, 2011|author=Becky Ashton|title=QPR 1 - 0 Chelsea|work=BBC Sport|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/eng_prem/15325522.stm|passage=Luiz struggled with the movement of Helguson in the box, as he collected a long ball and the Spaniard barged him over, leaving referee Chris Foy little '''option''' but to point to the spot.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20251010022722/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/eng_prem/15325522.stm|archivedate=10 October 2025}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2012-01|author=Steven Sloman|title=The Battle Between Intuition and Deliberation|volume=100|issue=1|page=74|magazine=w:American Scientist|url=http://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/the-battle-between-intuition-and-deliberation|passage=Libertarian paternalism is the view that, because the way '''options''' are presented to citizens affects what they choose, society should present '''options''' in a way that “nudges” our intuitive selves to make choices that are more consistent with what our more deliberative selves would have chosen if they were in control.|accessdate=12 May 2013|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20120108050233/http://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/the-battle-between-intuition-and-deliberation|archivedate=8 January 2012}}
# The [[freedom]] or [[right]] to choose.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1895|title=A Digest of Anglo-Muhammadan Law|author=Roland Knyvet Wilson|text=The marriage had not been contracted by the father, who was apparently dead, but by an agnatic guardian, so that it was liable to cancellation at the '''option''' of the girl on her attaining puberty.}}
# {{senseid|en|Q187860}}{{lb|en|finance|legal}} A [[contract]] giving the holder the right to buy or sell an [[asset]] at a set [[strike]] price; can apply to financial [[market]] [[transactions]], or to ordinary transactions for [[tangible]] assets such as a residence or automobile. {{defdate|from mid-18th c.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=13 Aug 77|volume=5|number=6|journal=Gay Community News|page=8|author=Pala Bennett|author2=Mary Jo Risher|author3=Ann Foreman|title="I'm A Mother, And I'm A Good One."|text=There's a book out on us and there's gonna be a movie based on the book. ABC has bought the '''option''' for a movie to be made specially for T.V.}}
# {{lb|en|law|uncountable}} The acquiring or retention of a [[nationality]] through personal choice as a [[right]], bypassing selective legal mechanisms for [[naturalization]], especially in cases where a territory is transferred or passed on from one state to another.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1928|title=Appeal to the Noble English Nation by the Committee of the Defenders of the Rights of Palestinian Arab Emigrants to the Palestinian Citizenship|page=10|text=At that time every emigrant who was made aware of such a notification submitted his application for '''option''' to the British Consuls within the period prescribed.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1998|author=w:Venice Commission|title=Consequences of State Succession for Nationality|isbn=9287138087|page=45|pageurl=https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=aXLc0jFYzogC&pg=PA45|text=A right of '''option''', mostly in favour of the nationality of the predecessor State, has been accorded in most cases of partial State succession, either by treaty or by domestic legislation.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Oliver W. Vonk|title=Dual Nationality in the European Union: A Study on Changing Norms{{nb...}}|isbn=9789004227200|page=243, note 154|pageurl=https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=GTYzAQAAQBAJ&pg=PA243|text=This group constitutes around 9 percent of the total number of acquisitions of Dutch nationality through '''option'''.}}

